  "rationale": "All three outputs put the audit first, separate static evidence from runtime hypotheses, claim no tool runs, and avoid redesign. B wins because it attaches acceptance criteria to each finding. It also has the most complete measurement-first gates (latency, long tasks, mounted rows, CLS, memory and regression budgets) and spells out rollback and safe-fallback conditions. A is close behind; it has the strongest detector reconciliation but thinner thresholds and less per-fix acceptance. C is solid but has the weakest performance budgets and fix-level acceptance.",
